  "name": "Question 5",
  "problem": "Consider the line $x=4$ and linear transformation $T$ with matrix representation $T,$ given by\n\n$$\nT=[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nIn **standard form**, the equation of the image of the line under the action of $T$ is\n\n{{field-1}}$=16.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, notice that the line $x=4$ passes through the points $G(4,0)$ and $H(4,1)$ since the coordinates of both points satisfy the equation.\n\nTo find the image of $G$ and $H$ under the action of $T$, we first create a matrix $X$ containing the coordinates of the points:\n\n$$\nX=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the image of $X$ under the action of $T$ by calculating the matrix product $TX:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}TX & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}4 & 4 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}12 & 10 \\\\ 4 & 6\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the coordinates of the respective image points are ${G}^{\u2032}(12,4)$ and ${H}^{\u2032}(10,6).$\n\nNow, we write the equation of the line that passes through ${G}^{\u2032}$ and ${H}^{\u2032}$ in point-slope form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-{y}_{1} & =\\frac{{y}_{2}-{y}_{1}}{{x}_{2}-{x}_{1}}(x-{x}_{1}) \\\\ y-4 & =\\frac{6-4}{10-12}(x-12) \\\\ y-4 & =-1(x-12)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we transform the equation of the line to standard form:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y-4 & =-x+12 \\\\ x+y & =16\\end{aligned}\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
